.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt GETDENTS64 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getdents64
.Nd read directory entries
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd "#define _GNU_SOURCE"
.Fd "#define _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE"
.In dirent.h
.Ft int
.Fn getdents64 "int fd" "struct dirent *dirp" "size_t count"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn getdents64
reads directory entries from
.Fa fd
into the
.Fa count Ns -byte
buffer at
.Fa dirp .
It returns complete, variable-length records and advances the cursor
in the descriptor's shared open-file description.
The next call continues from that cursor, which
.Xr lseek 2
sets back to 0, as
.Fn rewinddir
does, or to a
.Va d_off
a call gave, as
.Fn seekdir
does.
.Pp
musl defines
.Fn getdents64
as a macro for
.Fn getdents
when
.Dv _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE
is defined.
The declaration of
.Fn getdents
requires
.Dv _GNU_SOURCE
or
.Dv _BSD_SOURCE .
Its wrapper limits
.Fa count
to
.Dv INT_MAX
and invokes system call 220 on i386 and 217 on x86-64.
.Ss Record layout
Each record has the Linux
.Vt linux_dirent64
layout:
.Bl -tag -width "d_reclen"
.It Va d_ino
Unsigned 64-bit inode number at byte offset 0.
.It Va d_off
Signed 64-bit cursor for the next entry at byte offset 8.
Its meaning depends on the file system; it is not a byte offset into
the returned buffer.
.It Va d_reclen
Unsigned 16-bit length of this record at byte offset 16.
Advance by this value to reach the next record.
.It Va d_type
Unsigned 8-bit file type at byte offset 18.
.It Va d_name
Null-terminated name beginning at byte offset 19.
.El
.Pp
The record length is rounded up to a multiple of eight bytes.
Padding bytes are zero.
.Va d_type
is the entry's file type, the type bits of its mode shifted right by
twelve, as on Linux:
.Dv DT_FIFO
(1),
.Dv DT_CHR
(2),
.Dv DT_DIR
(4),
.Dv DT_BLK
(6),
.Dv DT_REG
(8),
.Dv DT_LNK
(10) or
.Dv DT_SOCK
(12), or
.Dv DT_UNKNOWN
(0) where the file system cannot tell.
It is never
.Dv DT_REG
for anything but a regular file.
FAT32 and exFAT have only directories and regular files.
tmpfs and KRFFS take the type from the node's mode.
ext2 takes the type from the entry's file-type byte when the volume has
the
.Li filetype
feature, and a byte of 0 or past 7 is
.Dv DT_UNKNOWN ;
without the feature it takes it from the inode's mode, where Linux
reports
.Dv DT_UNKNOWN .
Synthetic directories report
.Dv DT_DIR
for directories,
.Dv DT_REG
for
.Pa /proc
files,
.Dv DT_LNK
for
.Pa /proc/self ,
the
.Pa exe
links and the descriptor links,
.Dv DT_CHR
for a character device and
.Dv DT_BLK
for a disk node under
.Pa /dev .
.Ss File systems
KRFFS lists named children stored in the directory.
It omits unlinked files still held open and does not synthesize
.Ql \&.
or
.Ql .. .
Deleting entries between batches does not skip the surviving entries.
Creating or relocating a child during enumeration can cause that child
to be omitted or repeated.
.Pp
ext2 lists its stored directory entries, including
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql .. ,
and skips unused entries.
FAT32 lists live entries, using a valid long name when it fits or the
short name otherwise; it skips deleted entries and volume labels.
Its stored
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql ..
entries are returned when present.
exFAT lists valid file entry sets and does not synthesize dot entries.
.Pp
Every synthetic directory begins with
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql .. .
.Pa /dev
lists
.Pa shm ,
the tmpfs mounted there, then the published device registry entries.
.Pa /dev/pts
lists allocated pseudo-terminal slaves by number.
.Pa /proc
lists
.Pa cmdline ,
.Pa disk ,
.Pa uptime ,
.Pa stat ,
.Pa meminfo ,
.Pa mounts ,
.Pa net ,
.Pa self ,
.Pa usb ,
.Pa loop ,
.Pa console ,
and
.Pa audio ,
followed by process directories.
.Pa /proc/net
lists
.Pa dev ,
.Pa route ,
.Pa arp ,
and
.Pa tcp .
.Pa /proc/<pid>
lists
.Pa stat ,
.Pa cmdline ,
.Pa fd ,
.Pa maps ,
.Pa status ,
.Pa statm ,
.Pa auxv ,
.Pa environ ,
.Pa comm ,
.Pa mem ,
.Pa exe
and
.Pa task .
.Pa /proc/<pid>/task
lists the process's threads as directories named by thread ID, the
thread whose ID is the process ID first; a thread that has ended and
waits to be reaped is left out unless it is that one.
.Pa /proc/<pid>/task/<tid>
lists the entries of
.Pa /proc/<pid>
but
.Pa task .
.Pa /proc/<pid>/fd
lists that process's open descriptors as links named by descriptor number.
Enumeration of the root file system does not add synthetic mount names.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn getdents64
returns the number of bytes written, or 0 at the end of the directory.
If a directory read or user-memory copy fails after records have been
written, it returns the bytes already written.
On failure before any records are written, it returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/dirprobe.c
creates
.Pa /e/a
and
.Pa /e/b ,
opens
.Pa /e ,
and reads its entries into a 256-byte buffer.
It walks the records by
.Va d_reclen
and requires exactly the two names on KRFFS, with one
.Ql \&.
and one
.Ql ..
as well on ext2.
.Pp
.Pa tests/musl/direntmusl.c
requires every entry of
.Pa /dev ,
.Pa /proc ,
.Pa /proc/self ,
.Pa /proc/self/fd
and a directory of its own to report the type
.Xr lstat 2
gives, or
.Dv DT_UNKNOWN .
Given a directory on an ext2 volume, as
.Ic tests/run ext2
gives it
.Pa /tmp
on an ext2 root, it requires a symbolic link to be
.Dv DT_LNK
and checks
.Pa /
and
.Pa /bin
too.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOTDIR]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is invalid, was opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
or its open-file description has no mount.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
There is no current process, or
.Fa count
is not zero and
.Fa dirp
is null or the buffer is not writable user memory.
The kernel validates the whole requested buffer before reading entries.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The buffer cannot hold the next record and no record has been written,
or the file system has no directory-reading operation.
A
.Fa count
of zero is this error before the end of the directory and returns 0 at
the end, whatever
.Fa dirp
is, as on Linux.
.It Bq Er EIO
The root is not mounted, the backing device has been removed, or a
file-system read fails or encounters invalid directory data.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
The descriptor does not refer to a directory.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr lseek 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
